ChP 2025 Vol II: Water for Injection (WFI)

1. Statutory Production Innovation: Prepared from purified water by distillation OR equivalent membrane purification processes (RO + CEDI + Ultrafiltration). Distillation and membrane routes are officially equivalent in 2025 edition.

2. Specifications:
- In-line Conductivity: <= 1.3 uS/cm at 25 C;
- TOC <= 0.50 mg/L;
- Bacterial Endotoxins (ChP 1143): Less than 0.25 EU/ml;
- Bioburden: TAMC <= 10 CFU/100ml using membrane filtration on R2A agar.

ChP 2025 Vol II: Sterile Water for Injection

1. Definition: WFI sterilized in final containers. Used as vehicle/diluent for parenteral powders or injections.

2. Critical Limits:
- Sterility (ChP 1101): Complies with 14-day incubation incubation test in FTM and TSB;
- Bacterial Endotoxins: < 0.25 EU/ml;
- Sub-visible Particulate Matter (ChP 0903 Light Obscuration, containers < 100ml): Particles >= 10 um <= 6000/container; particles >= 25 um <= 600/container;
- pH range 5.0 to 7.0.

ChP 2025 Volume II: Aspirin & Enteric-Coated Tablets

1. Chemical Definition: 2-(Acetyloxy)benzoic acid (C9H8O4, MW 180.16, mp 135-140 C).

2. Free Salicylic Acid Impurity Limits (HPLC ChP 0512):
- API: Not more than 0.1% (<= 0.1%);
- Plain Tablets: Not more than 0.3%;
- Enteric-Coated Tablets: Not more than 1.5%.

3. Two-Stage Dissolution Test (ChP 0931 Basket Method):
- Acid Phase (0.1 M HCl, 2 hours): Release <= 10%;
- Buffer Phase (pH 6.8 Phosphate Buffer, 45 min): Release >= 75%.

ChP 2025 Volume II: Ibuprofen & Sustained-Release Capsules

1. Properties: 2-(4-isobutylphenyl)propanoic acid (C13H18O2, MW 206.28, mp 75.0-78.0 C).

2. Related Substances (HPLC ChP 0512):
- 4-Isobutylacetophenone (Impurity A, phototoxic): <= 0.10%;
- Any other single impurity <= 0.10%; Total impurities <= 0.50%.

3. Three-Stage Dissolution for Sustained-Release Formulations (pH 7.2 buffer, 50 rpm):
- 1 hour: 20% to 45% (prevents dose dumping);
- 4 hours: 50% to 75%;
- 8 hours: >= 80%.

ChP 2025 Volume II: Sodium Chloride (0.9%) and Glucose (5%, 10%) Injections

1. 0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection (Normal Saline):
- Osmolality (ChP 0632): 260 to 320 mOsmol/kg (isotonic);
- Bacterial Endotoxins (ChP 1143): < 0.50 EU/ml;
- Sub-visible Particles (ChP 0903, per ml): >= 10 um <= 25 particles/ml; >= 25 um <= 3 particles/ml;
- Sterility (ChP 1101): Complies with 14-day incubation test.

2. Glucose Injection:
- 5-Hydroxymethylfurfural (5-HMF): Absorbance at 284 nm <= 0.25;
- Endotoxins: < 0.25 EU/ml; pH 3.2 to 5.5.

ChP 2025 Volume III: Human Albumin

1. Manufacturing: Isolated from healthy human plasma via Cohn cold ethanol fractionation or validated chromatography, followed by terminal pasteurization at 60.0 C for 10 hours for viral inactivation.

2. Release Specifications:
- Albumin Purity (Cellulose acetate electrophoresis): Not less than 96.0% of total protein;
- Molecular Size Distribution (SEC HPLC): Monomer plus dimer >= 90.0%; Polymers <= 5.0%;
- Residual Aluminum (GFAAS): Not more than 200 ug/L to prevent encephalopathy in renal patients;
- Bacterial Endotoxins: Less than 5.0 EU per gram protein (< 5.0 EU/g);
- Sterility (ChP 1101): Complies with 14-day incubation.

ChP 2025 Volume III: Recombinant Human Insulin

1. Molecular Characteristics: 51-amino acid two-chain peptide (A chain 21, B chain 30, MW 5807.57) expressed in E. coli or yeast.

2. Potency and Assay (HPLC):
Calculated on dried basis: Not less than 27.5 IU per mg (>= 27.5 IU/mg).

3. Related Proteins and Purity (RP-HPLC & SEC):
- High Molecular Weight Proteins (HMWP): Not more than 0.30% (<= 0.30%);
- A21 Desamido Insulin: Not more than 2.0%;
- Total other related proteins: Not more than 1.5%;
- Zinc content: 0.30% to 1.00%;
- Bacterial Endotoxins: < 0.80 EU per 100 IU.

ChP 2025 Volume III: Trastuzumab for Injection (Anti-HER2 mAb)

1. Description: Recombinant humanized IgG1k monoclonal antibody produced in CHO cells targeting the extracellular domain of HER2 (ErbB2), MW ~148 kDa.

2. Critical Quality Attributes (CQAs):
- Purity (CE-SDS ChP 0542): Non-reduced main IgG peak >= 95.0%; Reduced heavy + light chain >= 98.0%;
- Size Variants (SEC-HPLC): Monomer >= 98.0%; Aggregates <= 1.5%; Fragments <= 0.5%;
- Charge Heterogeneity (CEX-HPLC / iCIEF): Main charge peak >= 55.0-65.0%;
- N-Glycan Profile: G0F, G1F, G2F afucosylation and galactosylation consistency with reference;
- Biological Activity: HER2 binding assay 80% to 125% relative to reference standard;
- Endotoxins: < 0.25 EU per mg; Sterility compliant.

Chinese Pharmacopoeia 2025 General Chapter 0101: Tablets

1. Disintegration Time Standards (ChP 0921 at 37 C):
- Plain uncoated tablets: Fully disintegrated within 15 minutes (<= 15 min);
- Film-coated tablets: Within 30 minutes; Sugar-coated: Within 60 minutes;
- Enteric-coated tablets: No crack or softening in 0.1 M HCl for 2 hours; fully disintegrated in pH 6.8 buffer within 60 minutes;
- Dispersible tablets: Within 3 minutes at 15-25 C passing No. 2 sieve.

2. Friability Test (ChP 0923, 100 revolutions):
Weight loss shall not exceed 1.0% (<= 1.0%) with no broken or chipped tablets.

3. Uniformity of Dosage Units (ChP 0941): Mandatory for units < 25 mg or drug loading < 25%, acceptance value A+2.2S <= 15.0.

Chinese Pharmacopoeia 2025 General Chapter 0261: Pharmaceutical Water

1. Categories: Drinking Water, Purified Water, Water for Injection (WFI), and Sterile Water for Injection.
Major Compendial Innovation: WFI may be produced via distillation OR validated membrane processes (RO + CEDI + Ultrafiltration).

2. Engineering & Piping Design Specifications:
- Material: 316L stainless steel with electropolished inner surface (Ra <= 0.4 um);
- Dead-leg Rule: Branch dead-legs must adhere to <= 3D rule (length <= 3x diameter, preferably <= 1.5D);
- Flow Velocity: Minimum return loop velocity >= 1.0 m/s ensuring turbulent flow (Re > 4000);
- Thermal Sanitation: Continuous hot circulation >= 70 C, or routine SIP / ozone sanitation.

ChP 2025 General Chapter 0512: High Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC)

System Suitability Test Parameters:
1. Theoretical Plates (N): N = 5.54 * (tR / Wh/2)^2, meeting specified lower limits;
2. Resolution (R): R = 2 * (tR2 - tR1) / (W1 + W2). Unless otherwise specified, R shall be >= 1.5 ensuring baseline separation;
3. Tailing Factor (T): T = W0.05h / (2 * d1). Between 0.95 and 1.05 for symmetrical peaks (0.80-1.50 for impurity tests);
4. Precision (RSD): Relative standard deviation of peak areas across 5 or 6 consecutive injections shall not exceed 2.0% (RSD <= 2.0%).

ChP 2025 General Chapter 0861: Residual Solvents (Harmonized with ICH Q3C)

Headspace Gas Chromatography (HS-GC) Classification & Limits:
1. Class 1 Solvents (Avoid Use - Carcinogens):
- Benzene <= 2 ppm;
- Carbon tetrachloride <= 4 ppm;
- 1,2-Dichloroethane <= 5 ppm;
2. Class 2 Solvents (Limited Use per PDE):
- Acetonitrile: PDE 4.1 mg/day, <= 410 ppm;
- Methanol: PDE 30.0 mg/day, <= 3000 ppm;
- Dichloromethane: PDE 6.0 mg/day, <= 600 ppm;
- Toluene: PDE 8.9 mg/day, <= 890 ppm;
3. Class 3 Solvents (Low Toxicity): Ethanol, acetone, ethyl acetate, isopropanol <= 5000 ppm (0.5%).

ChP 2025 General Chapter 0931: Dissolution & Drug Release

1. Compendial Apparatus:
- Method 1 (Basket, 100 rpm) for floating dosage forms/capsules;
- Method 2 (Paddle, 50-75 rpm) standard for solid orals;
- Method 4 (Flow-through cell) for poorly soluble drugs, maintained at 37 C +/- 0.5 C.

2. Similarity Factor f2 for In Vitro Equivalence:
f2 = 50 * log10 { [ 1 + (1/n) * sum( (Rt - Tt)^2 ) ]^(-0.5) * 100 }
Criterion: Two dissolution curves are deemed equivalent if 50 <= f2 <= 100.

NMPA 2022 Device Software Guideline: Versioning & COTS Management

1. Software Version Naming Rules: Registrant must define naming conventions. Released software version is documented on the registration certificate.

2. Major Software Updates (Requires Regulatory Change Application):
- Modification of intended use;
- Substantial change in core algorithms (e.g. AI retraining) or clinical functionality;
- Operating environment changes increasing clinical risks;
- Architectural overhaul or safety mechanism redesign.
Minor updates (bug fixes, cosmetics) are maintained under QMS and reported upon renewal.

3. COTS / SOUP Governance: Registrant assumes total responsibility for third-party software, requiring known vulnerability tracking, residual risk analysis, and maintenance obsolescence plans.

NMPA CMDE Announcement No. 7 of 2022: Medical Device Cybersecurity Review Guideline (2022 Revision)

1. Scope: Applies to Class II and Class III medical devices featuring electronic data exchange, remote access, or user authentication interfaces.

2. 22 Core Cybersecurity Capabilities:
- Automatic logout upon inactivity timeout;
- Role-based access control (RBAC) and strong authentication (no default passwords);
- Tamper-proof, immutable audit trail logging all safety events and PHI access;
- Cryptographic security: In-transit TLS 1.2/1.3 and at-rest AES-256 encryption;
- Anti-malware defenses, application whitelisting, input validation;
- Incident response, backup recovery, and fail-safe clinical fallback mode.

NMPA Announcement No. 68 of 2020: TCM Registration Classification

1. Four Statutory TCM Categories:
- Category 1: Innovative Traditional Chinese Medicines (TCM);
- Category 2: Modified TCM (new delivery route, new dosage form, new indication);
- Category 3: Ancient Classical Prescriptions (exempted from clinical trials per official compendium);
- Category 4: Generic / Same Name & Same Formula TCM.

2. 'Three-in-One' Regulatory Evidence Model: Synergistic integration of TCM Theory, Historical Human Experience (Real-World Evidence), and Confirmatory Clinical Trials.

China GCP (2020 Revision, NMPA & NHC Order No. 57)

1. Ethics Committee & Informed Consent: Institutional Review Board independence; voluntary signed informed consent is mandatory prior to any protocol procedures.

2. Serious Adverse Event (SAE) Reporting Timelines:
- Investigator to Sponsor: Written report within 24 hours of awareness;
- Fatal / Life-Threatening SUSAR: Sponsor reports to NMPA CDE within 7 calendar days, with full followup within additional 8 days;
- Other SUSAR: Sponsor reports within 15 calendar days.

National Medical Products Administration (NMPA) Institutional Overview & Statutory Mandate

1. Agency Profile: Under the State Administration for Market Regulation (SAMR), NMPA regulates drugs, medical devices, and cosmetics in China (successor to CFDA/SFDA following 2018 institutional reform).

2. Statutory Authorities: Drug Administration Law, Vaccine Administration Law, Medical Device Supervision and Administration Regulations (Order No. 739), Cosmetics Regulations.

3. Core Mandates:
- Formulating regulations and promulgating national standards including Chinese Pharmacopoeia (ChP);
- Premarket evaluation, clinical trial approvals (IND), and marketing authorizations (NDA/BLA/ANDA/Medical Device Registration);
- Lifecycle quality supervision across GxP (GLP, GCP, GMP, GSP);
- Post-marketing pharmacovigilance (ADR) and adverse event monitoring;
- International regulatory harmonization (ICH, IMDRF, PIC/S).

NMPA Departments & Technical Centers:

1. Internal Regulatory Departments: Drug Registration Dept, Drug Inspection Dept, Medical Device Registration Dept, Medical Device Inspection Dept, Cosmetics Dept.

2. Affiliated Technical Centers:
- CDE (Center for Drug Evaluation): Lead technical review body for human drugs (IND, NDA, ANDA, BE evaluation);
- CMDE (Center for Medical Device Evaluation): Technical review body for Class III, imported devices, and IVDs;
- CFDI (Center for Food and Drug Inspection): National inspectorate conducting GMP, GCP, GLP, and GSP onsite audits;
- NIFDC (National Institutes for Food and Drug Control): Statutory reference laboratory, testing, and lot release of biologics;
- CDR (Center for Drug Reevaluation / Adverse Reaction Monitoring): National pharmacovigilance center.

FDA Final Guidance (June 14, 2023): Device Software Functions Documentation

1. Complete Replacement of Legacy Level of Concern (LOC):
Replaces the 2005 Minor/Moderate/Major LOC framework with a streamlined two-tier structure: Basic Documentation and Enhanced Documentation.

2. Enhanced Documentation Criteria (Any factor triggers Enhanced tier):
- Constituent of a Combination Product;
- Used in transfusion medicine or tissue donation eligibility;
- Intended for life-supporting or life-sustaining functions;
- Failure or design flaw presents a risk of serious injury or death.

3. Basic Documentation: Applies to all other software functions not meeting Enhanced criteria.

Title 21 CFR Part 11: Electronic Records; Electronic Signatures

1. Scope & Equivalence (§ 11.1): Electronic records and signatures meeting Part 11 criteria are legally equivalent to traditional paper records and handwritten signatures.

2. Controls for Closed Systems (§ 11.10):
- System Validation: Documented verification of software accuracy, reliability, and consistency;
- Immutable Audit Trail (§ 11.10(e)): Computer-generated, time-stamped audit trails independently recording operator entries and actions. Audit records cannot be altered or deleted by any user (including administrators);
- Operational authority and device checks.

U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) Overview & Statutory Mandate

1. Mission & Statutory Authority (21 U.S.C. § 393 & PHS Act):
The FDA is an agency within the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS). Its statutory mission is to protect public health by ensuring the safety, efficacy, and security of human and veterinary drugs, biological products, and medical devices, and by ensuring the safety of food supply, cosmetics, and radiation-emitting products.

2. Regulatory Scope:
- Human Drugs: Prescription (Rx), Over-the-Counter (OTC), and generic medicines under the FD&C Act;
- Biologics: Vaccines, blood, monoclonal antibodies, gene & cell therapies under PHS Act § 351;
- Medical Devices: Class I, II, III devices, SaMD, and radiation-emitting products under FD&C Act §§ 510, 513, 515, 524B;
- Food safety, cosmetics, veterinary drugs, and tobacco products.

FDA Organizational Centers & Review Pathways:

1. Core Operating Centers:
- CDER (Center for Drug Evaluation and Research): Regulates small molecules, OTC, prescription drugs, and therapeutic proteins;
- CBER (Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research): Regulates vaccines, blood products, gene and cell therapies;
- CDRH (Center for Devices and Radiological Health): Regulates medical devices, SaMD/AI software, IVDs, and radiological products;
- ORA (Office of Regulatory Affairs): Lead office for all agency field inspections, cGMP audits, and import oversight.

2. Key Regulatory Pathways:
- Drugs/Biologics: IND (clinical investigation), NDA (new drug approval), ANDA (generic bioequivalence), BLA (biologic license);
- Devices: 510(k) (substantial equivalence), De Novo (low/moderate risk novel devices), PMA (high-risk Class III premarket approval);
- Post-market: MedWatch reporting, FAERS, and MAUDE surveillance.

MDCG 2019-11: Four-Step MDSW Qualification Process

Step 1: Verify software form factor (cloud, mobile app, firmware);
Step 2: Medical Purpose Check per MDR Article 2(1) (administrative, lifestyle, or billing tools excluded);
Step 3: Action on Data for Benefit of Individual Patients (algorithms transforming or interpreting data rather than passive storage/transmission);
Step 4: Classification under Annex VIII Rule 11 establishing Notified Body involvement.

ICH Q10: Pharmaceutical Quality System (PQS)

1. Four Lifecycle Stages: Pharmaceutical Development, Technology Transfer, Commercial Manufacturing, Product Discontinuation.

2. Two Enablers: Knowledge Management (KM) and Quality Risk Management (QRM per ICH Q9).

3. Four Core QMS Elements:
- Process Performance and Product Quality Monitoring System;
- Corrective Action and Preventive Action (CAPA) System;
- Change Management System;
- Management Review of Process Performance and Product Quality.
